    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID43",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Qbae15cb77558f324-chunk_e1e052a49af18652162a855a",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.1 descriptive attacks</strong> – Characterising or describing women in a derogatory manner. This could include, but not limited to: negative generalisations about women’s abilities, appearance, sexual behaviour, intellect, character, or morals.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p>Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles<br><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Women as hypersexualised, and their bodies for the pleasure and spectacle of men.\n\nDouble standards regarding nudity:\n - Censorship of women’s bodies which are considered sexualised and indecent as compared to men’s, especially on social media and online platforms.\n - Women’s bodies expected to meet specific beauty standards but considered lewd in their natural state (e.g. during menstruation, showing bodily hair, breastfeeding, diverse body shapes and skin colours).\n\nPolicing of women and girl’s clothing and the idea that certain styles of dress are distracting to men and boys.\n\nBlaming and/or stigmatization and shaming of sexual violence survivors, and women and girls who report sexual violence as promiscuous, liars or ‘out to get’ the perpetrator.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Qbae15cb77558f324-chunk_e1e052a49af18652162a855a",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
