    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID51",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q9d1329bac1b971b9-chunk_5437e27e98d60e6f2a587419",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.2 aggressive and emotive attacks</strong> – Expressing strong negative sentiment against women, such as disgust or hatred. This can be through direct description of the speaker’s subjective emotions, baseless accusations, or the use of gendered slurs, gender-based profanities and gender-based insults.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p>Impact of sexist hate speech on women and girls<br><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Sexist hate speech is often treated as a harmless and non-serious issue and women are explicitly or implicitly told to bear with it. However, not only does sexist hate speech undermine freedom of speech for women and girls, but its psychological, emotional and/or physical impacts are real and severe. The aim of sexist hate speech is to humiliate or objectify women, to undervalue their skills and opinions, to destroy their reputation, to make them feel vulnerable and fearful, and to control and punish them for not following a certain behaviour. Sexist hate speech has the effect of silencing women, obliging them to adapt their behaviour and limit their movements and participation in diverse human activities.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q9d1329bac1b971b9-chunk_5437e27e98d60e6f2a587419",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
